{"id":"2026-11-22-UNM","date":"2026-11-22","ticker":"UNM","name":"Unum Group","sector":"Financials","industry":"Insurance - Life","price":75.9,"price_date":"2026-11-20","timeframe_days":182,"headline":"Unum cut its 2026 earnings outlook after disability claims ran far above plan, and new reinsurance deals will cost about $90M to $100M a quarter","side":"short","timeframe":"About 6 months, through the next two quarterly reports","thesis":"Unum's adjusted earnings per share fell to $2.20 in the September quarter from $2.81 a year ago, and that $2.20 includes $0.33 of one-off gains. The core problem is disability claims. The group benefit ratio, the share of premiums paid out as claims, was 70.3% against an outlook of 62% to 64% at the start of the year, and the CFO tied the rise in long-term disability claims to behavioral health claims that tend to climb when the job market weakens. Management cut its 2026 adjusted earnings per share outlook from $8.60 to $8.90 down to $8.30 to $8.50, and that new range also includes the $0.33 of one-off gains. On top of that, reinsurance deals to shed its old long-term-care book will cost about $90M to $100M a quarter at first. Sales fell from a year ago. The stock dropped 18% on November 4 and has kept sliding since, which suggests the market has not finished repricing a slower earnings path.","wrong_if":"A close above 84 would retake the level where the November 5 bounce stalled and mean the selling is over. The idea is also wrong if the group benefit ratio falls back to the mid-60s in the December quarter, or if Unum guides 2027 earnings growth near its usual 8% to 12%.","business":"Unum sells insurance mostly through employers. Its main products are disability insurance, which replaces part of a worker's pay if they get sick or hurt and cannot work, plus group life, dental, vision and accident coverage. Colonial Life sells similar policies to workers at their jobs. It also runs in the UK and Poland. A closed block of old long-term-care policies, which it no longer sells, is being moved to reinsurers.","segments_note":"The brief has no segment table, so this comes from the call. Unum US group disability had the worst quarter: the claim ratio rose about 5 points from long-term disability claims and about 2 points from short-term disability and paid family leave. Colonial Life did well, with a 49% benefit ratio on good claims in individual disability and dental, and premiums grew 3%. The weakness is in the largest business, and the good parts were not enough to offset it.","growth_note":"Reported revenue fell 25% because of the long-term-care reinsurance deal, which is an accounting effect, not lost business. Premium growth is still about 4% to 7% for the year. The real slowdown is in earnings: adjusted earnings per share were $2.20 against $2.81 a year ago, and the GAAP result was a loss of $2.65 a share from the reinsurance charge. The brief's earnings growth column shows +5.3%, which disagrees with the CFO's numbers; this idea uses the call.","commentary":[{"point":"Adjusted earnings per share were $2.20, including $0.33 of one-off gains, against $2.81 a year ago, which included $0.75 of one-offs.","speaker":"Steven Zabel, CFO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"The group benefit ratio was 70.3%, higher than expected, with nearly 8 points coming from long-term disability claims and paid family leave they are working to reprice.","speaker":"Steven Zabel, CFO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"Now expect 2026 adjusted earnings per share of $8.30 to $8.50, including $0.33 of one-off gains. The prior range was $8.60 to $8.90.","speaker":"Steven Zabel, CFO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"Reinsurance deals will cost about $90M to $100M a quarter at first, then slowly decline.","speaker":"Steven Zabel, CFO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"Said the rest of the business showed its earning power but was not enough to overcome the disability headwinds.","speaker":"Richard McKenney, CEO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"tone"},{"point":"Linked the rise in disability claims to behavioral health claims, which tend to rise when the job market is uncertain, a sign the problem may not be one quarter.","speaker":"Steven Zabel, CFO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"tone"},{"point":"Leaned on strong capital generation and buybacks, which is the main support for the stock.","speaker":"Richard McKenney, CEO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"tone"}],"tone_note":"This was the biggest tone drop in the batch. Net tone fell from 99 to 30, a drop of 69 points, the answers to analysts were 36 points cooler, and management dodged one question. Words like pressure and headwinds replaced the upbeat language of the spring, when the CEO said he was confident in the full-year outlook he has now cut.","technical_note":"The stock closed at 75.90, below its 50-day average (88.28) and 200-day average (84.22). It fell from about 91 to 75 on November 4, bounced to about 81 the next day, and has slid back since. RSI is 32, low but not extreme. The 52-week low is 69.02. Volume over the last 20 days is 19% above normal, a sign of steady selling.","valuation_note":"At 9.0 times adjusted earnings, Unum is at its median of 9.1, so it is not stretched either way. The trailing earnings still include the stronger first half and the one-off gains. The 20th percentile of 6.7 shows how low insurers trade when claims go wrong.","levels":{"entry_lo":74,"entry_hi":80,"stop":84,"target":62,"note":"Sell short between 74 and 80, from the current price up to the November 5 bounce high of about 81. The stop at 84 is above that bounce and about three daily ranges above the posted price. The target of 62 is under the 52-week low of 69 and works out to about 7.5 times the cut earnings outlook. From the posted price that is about $14 of downside for $8 of risk, or 1.7 to 1."},"risks":["Buybacks: Unum generates about $1.3B of capital a year and returns most of it, which supports the stock.","The stock is cheap; a quick return to normal claims would make the cut look like a one-time scare.","Repricing of disability policies could work faster than expected, since most group contracts reset each year.","Shorts of cheap insurers can rally hard on takeover talk or a big buyback.","Not known from the brief: the 2027 outlook, which Unum usually gives at a December or January investor meeting, and the size of the remaining long-term-care risk."],"catalysts":["Unum's 2027 outlook meeting, usually in December, where a weak earnings growth target would confirm the idea.","The December quarter report, likely early February 2027, with the next group benefit ratio.","Monthly jobs reports, since management tied disability claims to labor market strain."],"facts":{"financials":{"updated":"2026-11-19","adj_eps_name":"After-tax adjusted operating EPS","quarters":[{"q":"Dec '23","id":"FY2023Q4","end":"2023-12-31","rev":3145.1,"gm":null,"om":null,"nm":10.5,"eps":1.69,"adj_eps":1.79,"rev_yoy":4.6,"eps_yoy":25.2},{"q":"Mar '24","id":"FY2024Q1","end":"2024-03-31","rev":3200.3,"gm":null,"om":null,"nm":12.3,"eps":2.04,"adj_eps":2.12,"rev_yoy":5.4,"eps_yoy":13.4},{"q":"Jun '24","id":"FY2024Q2","end":"2024-06-30","rev":3233.4,"gm":null,"om":null,"nm":12.0,"eps":2.05,"adj_eps":2.16,"rev_yoy":3.9,"eps_yoy":4.9},{"q":"Sep '24","id":"FY2024Q3","end":"2024-09-30","rev":3217,"gm":null,"om":15.6,"nm":20.1,"eps":3.46,"adj_eps":2.13,"rev_yoy":4.0,"eps_yoy":9.8},{"q":"Dec '24","id":"FY2024Q4","end":"2024-12-31","rev":3236.6,"gm":null,"om":null,"nm":10.8,"eps":1.92,"adj_eps":2.03,"rev_yoy":2.9,"eps_yoy":13.4},{"q":"Mar '25","id":"FY2025Q1","end":"2025-03-31","rev":3091.6,"gm":null,"om":null,"nm":6.1,"eps":1.06,"adj_eps":2.04,"rev_yoy":-3.4,"eps_yoy":-3.8},{"q":"Jun '25","id":"FY2025Q2","end":"2025-06-30","rev":3361.4,"gm":null,"om":null,"nm":10.0,"eps":1.92,"adj_eps":2.07,"rev_yoy":4.0,"eps_yoy":-4.2},{"q":"Sep '25","id":"FY2025Q3","end":"2025-09-30","rev":3378.4,"gm":null,"om":null,"nm":1.2,"